WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.307
IoT devices are physical products,

2
00:00:02.387 --> 00:00:05.217
so hardware interfaces are part of the
security design.

3
00:00:05.297 --> 00:00:09.152
Debug ports, serial consoles, maintenance
connectors, buttons, sensors,

4
00:00:09.232 --> 00:00:12.002
actuators, buses, removable storage,
factory modes,

5
00:00:12.082 --> 00:00:16.237
and diagnostic commands may all influence
device behavior or reveal operational

6
00:00:16.317 --> 00:00:16.679
state.

7
00:00:16.759 --> 00:00:18.625
Debug ports need production review.

8
00:00:18.705 --> 00:00:21.476
Development access can be essential during
bring-up,

9
00:00:21.556 --> 00:00:25.229
but release builds should document which
access remains, what it can do,

10
00:00:25.309 --> 00:00:27.718
and how production configuration is
verified.

11
00:00:27.798 --> 00:00:30.448
The point is not to hide useful tools from
engineers;

12
00:00:30.528 --> 00:00:34.262
it is to avoid leaving broad maintenance
capability enabled by accident.

13
00:00:34.342 --> 00:00:36.811
Factory modes should not become field
exposure.

14
00:00:36.891 --> 00:00:40.444
Manufacturing workflows may need
calibration commands, test loops,

15
00:00:40.524 --> 00:00:42.933
provisioning paths, or detailed
diagnostics.

16
00:00:43.013 --> 00:00:47.289
Those features should have build-profile
rules and release checks so a device in the

17
00:00:47.369 --> 00:00:50.140
field does not behave like a device on the
factory line.

18
00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.568
Local interfaces require clear access
rules.

19
00:00:52.648 --> 00:00:55.780
A button sequence, removable media flow,
service connector,

20
00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.751
or nearby radio workflow may be
appropriate for support,

21
00:00:58.831 --> 00:01:02.564
but the product should define when it is
allowed, what state it can change,

22
00:01:02.644 --> 00:01:06.679
what evidence is recorded, and how it is
disabled or restricted when necessary.

23
00:01:06.759 --> 00:01:09.168
Sensor and actuator commands need
validation.

24
00:01:09.248 --> 00:01:11.838
Physical input may be noisy, stale,
disconnected,

25
00:01:11.918 --> 00:01:13.905
or inconsistent with the current mode.

26
00:01:13.985 --> 00:01:17.478
Commands that influence outputs should
check ranges, mode, timing,

27
00:01:17.558 --> 00:01:21.473
and authorization state where relevant
before changing physical behavior.

28
00:01:21.553 --> 00:01:24.624
Maintenance features should be documented
and controlled.

29
00:01:24.704 --> 00:01:27.956
A short table of interfaces, commands,
build profiles, owners,

30
00:01:28.036 --> 00:01:30.385
and production status can prevent
confusion.

31
00:01:30.465 --> 00:01:34.500
Hardware security is strongest when teams
know what remains available and why.
